item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6, 9 and 10 distinguish documented procedures, score precision and intended interpretation. Reinterpreting test–retest as repeated evaluation of an unchanged AI system, repetition and stability are unestablished. Relevant precision evidence would receive 0, meaning insufficient information, not demonstrated inadequacy. The reported score describes this run.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repetition is neither documented nor explicitly denied."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The tested system scored 70% in one reported run.",
          "reason": "One reported score does not establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §§6 and 9, repetition is explicitly absent and repeatability remains unestablished. Precision evidence would receive 0 rather than 1: an absent study does not demonstrate unstable scores. Section 10 limits the supported interpretation to observed grid-puzzle performance.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly denies repeated evaluation."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "No repeated scores establish stability; this also does not establish instability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9, interpreted as repeated evaluation of the same AI configuration, supports consistency under the documented conditions and stated stability definition. The range is descriptive precision evidence, not a reliability coefficient to which EFPA correlation anchors apply. Section 10 does not extend this finding beyond the evaluated puzzles and conditions.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "Repeated scores were evaluated with the relevant conditions held constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%. The report defines stable for this comparison as a range of at most two percentage points.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the report's explicit stability definition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 distinguishes conducting a precision study from obtaining satisfactory precision. Repetition is documented, but the results fail the stated stability requirement and demonstrate inadequate repeatability for that requirement. Section 10 therefore requires caution when interpreting a single score for comparisons.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "The developers evaluated repeated runs under unchanged conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%. The report defines stable for this comparison as a range of at most two percentage points.",
          "reason": "The observed variation explicitly exceeds the stated stability limit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.3.8 and 7 distinguish installation support from measurement evidence. Installation instructions add procedural information but cannot establish precision under §9 or strengthen score validity under §10 without relevant findings. Repetition and stability remain unestablished; missing precision evidence warrants 0, not demonstrated inadequacy.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "The installation update supplies no evidence that repetition occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "Installation guidance adds no repeated-score evidence supporting stability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA Part A identifies a narrow performance-comparison purpose. Under §§6 and 10, the observed scores support that comparison, while cross-domain intelligence lacks supporting content coverage or external validation. Section 9 precision is unreported. Under §8.3.2.2, norms can be inapplicable to this explicitly restricted raw-score comparison; no broader interpretation follows.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "A solved more of the evaluated puzzles under the stated scoring and instructions."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The findings do not establish comparative general intelligence across domains."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 evaluates the claimed use: selecting for general intelligence across domains requires evidence beyond the supplied grid-puzzle results. Sections 6 and 10 expose a gap between content coverage and intended interpretation; §9 precision is also unreported. The narrow observed comparison remains supported. Section 8's restricted-ranking exception cannot justify the broader selection claim.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "The reported evaluation directly supports better observed grid-puzzle performance by A."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "Declaring a broad intended use supplies no evidence validating that interpretation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§10.3.1.6 and 10.3.2 distinguish test scores from experimental evidence relating assistance to actual correctness. Random allocation, blinded checking and the reported interval support the later workshop-specific comparison. The 120 inspectors are trial participants, not an AI norm sample. Safety, time, broader generalisation and §9 repeatability remain unestablished; a full validity rating requires more methodological detail.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "The initial scores alone provide no demonstrated link to improved real workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%. The reported uncertainty interval for the difference was 12 to 28 percentage points.",
          "reason": "The randomised trial supports greater correctness with A within the studied workshop and conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "The trial supplies no measured safety outcome establishing improvement."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "The request to apply the complete original EFPA model is read alongside the explicit exemption from full questionnaires: all Parts A–C were considered, with relevant provisions discussed per case. Test taker and population are reinterpreted as AI system/configuration and intended system population; human age, handedness, sensory or motor disability, clinical qualifications and debriefing questions are inapplicable to AI examinees. Human operator accessibility and stakeholder guidance may remain relevant. Sections 4 and 12 report availability is unspecified; section 5 supply information, section 7 material quality and section 11 fair-use evidence are largely unreported. These gaps do not demonstrate poor quality. No report samples were supplied, so report quality cannot be rated; paper-and-pencil materials are inapplicable to the described AI evaluations. Part C uses only the supplied EFPA 2025 model and case passages. Claim labels are separate from EFPA ratings; no aggregate score or EFPA endorsement is implied. Under EFPA guidance, critical precision or validity evidence rated 0 limits use to research or exceptional expert use until supported. No other instruction required clarification."
}
